nts toward Democrats, leaving a D+2 lean with no incumbent on the ballot", "All nine expert ratings call a Democratic flip, with a consensus around Likely D", "Von Wilpert holds a heavy outside-spending edge (about $2.2M supporting her vs. $66K for Desmond)", "No general-election polls are available; a July report described the race as neck-and-neck"], "rationale": "The redrawn open seat leans Democratic, every major rater projects a Democratic flip, and outside spending and party investment heavily favor von Wilpert; Desmond's solid direct fundraising and the absence of polling keep uncertainty elevated but do not overturn the Likely Democratic picture.", "watch": "Fresh general-election polling showing Desmond running even among the district's moderate voters would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Open seat: Darrell Issa (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "California's 48th is an open seat reshaped by the Prop 50 redistricting, which moved the district roughly 18 points toward Democrats and left it with a slight Democratic lean. Darrell Issa's retirement removed the incumbent advantage Republicans held here, and the general election pits San Diego-area supervisor Jim Desmond against councilwoman Marni von Wilpert.\n\nRaters across the spectrum call this a Democratic flip, most rating it Likely or Lean Democratic, and prediction markets price von Wilpert near 90 percent. Von Wilpert benefits from roughly $2.2 million in outside support and a visit from the DCCC chair, while Republican outside groups have spent little. Desmond has kept pace in direct fundraising, and a July report described the race as neck-and-neck, but no general-election polls are available. Fundamentals and expert consensus point to a Democratic pickup with moderate uncertainty.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9032, "margin": 8.24}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p